---
import UseCasePage from '../../components/UseCasePage.astro';
---

<UseCasePage
  title="Docker Secrets & Config Management"
  description="Get secrets out of .env files: AES-256-GCM encryption at rest, templated config files with reusable fragments, usage tracking, and atomic sync to every Docker server."
  framing="Out of .env files, encrypted at rest"
  intro={[
    'The default state of Docker secrets on a fleet is quietly terrible: plaintext .env files copied to each host, drifting from each other, readable by anyone with SSH, and referenced by nothing that can tell you where a credential is actually used — which makes rotation a guessing game.',
    'BridgePort replaces that with an encrypted, environment-scoped store wired directly into config management. Secrets are AES-256-GCM encrypted at rest with a master key you provide; config files are composed from reusable fragments and templated per server; and every secret knows exactly which files reference it.',
  ]}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Store secrets and variables per environment',
      text: 'Secrets are encrypted with AES-256-GCM before they touch the database; revealing a decrypted value is restricted to admins, and sensitive operations are audit-logged.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Compose config from fragments',
      text: 'Reusable, environment-scoped fragments share common blocks across config files. Files can iterate tagged servers with range templating — one source renders correctly for every host.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Reference secrets in config files',
      text: 'Usage is tracked in both directions: list which files use a secret before rotating it, and see what a file depends on before touching it.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Sync atomically',
      text: 'Config rollouts run as all-or-nothing sync batches with dry-run previews and rollback. Editing a shared fragment auto-resyncs every dependent file; file edits keep history for rollback.',
    },
  ]}
  features={[
    {
      title: 'AES-256-GCM at rest',
      text: 'Secrets are encrypted with a master key you control; the database never stores plaintext values.',
      href: '/guides/secrets/',
      linkText: 'Secrets & variables',
    },
    {
      title: 'Admin-only reveal',
      text: 'Viewer and operator roles can use secrets in deployments without ever being able to read the decrypted values.',
      href: '/guides/users/',
      linkText: 'Users & roles',
    },
    {
      title: 'Usage tracking',
      text: 'Join tables map secrets and variables to the config files that reference them — rotation stops being archaeology.',
      href: '/guides/secrets/',
      linkText: 'Secrets guide',
    },
    {
      title: 'Reusable fragments',
      text: 'Shared config blocks defined once and included everywhere, with automatic resync of dependents on change.',
      href: '/guides/config-files/',
      linkText: 'Config files',
    },
    {
      title: 'Atomic sync batches',
      text: 'Multi-file, multi-server config rollouts are transactional: preview with dry-run, apply all-or-nothing, roll back if needed.',
      href: '/guides/config-files/',
      linkText: 'Config sync',
    },
    {
      title: 'Encrypted SSH keys too',
      text: 'Per-environment SSH keys are stored encrypted with the same machinery that protects your secrets.',
      href: '/operations/security/',
      linkText: 'Security & hardening',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'How are secrets encrypted?',
      a: 'With AES-256-GCM, using a master key you provide via environment variable when running BridgePort. Values are encrypted before storage and decrypted only when needed for a deployment or an admin-initiated reveal.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can my whole team see secret values?',
      a: 'No. Revealing decrypted secret values is an admin-only operation. Other roles can reference secrets in services and config without read access to the values, and reveals are audit-logged.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What happens when I edit a fragment used by many config files?',
      a: 'Dependent files are automatically resynced so servers never run a stale mix. Sync batches make multi-file rollouts atomic, with dry-run previews and rollback.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How do I know where a secret is used before rotating it?',
      a: 'BridgePort tracks which config files reference each secret and variable, so the blast radius of a rotation is a list you can read, not something you discover in production.',
    },
  ]}
  related={[
    { href: '/guides/secrets/', text: 'Secrets & variables' },
    { href: '/guides/config-files/', text: 'Config files' },
    { href: '/operations/security/', text: 'Security & hardening' },
  ]}
/>
